  "headline": "Oil shock, not reflation: markets are pricing stagflation with the dollar as the haven",
  "body": [
    "The dashboard reads as a stagflationary squeeze rather than a growth boom. Brent is up 80% year to date and the 10-year yield sits above 5.1%, nearly 100 basis points higher than at the start of the year. The driver is supply, not demand: Drop Site News frames the week as Iran forcing oil and inflation up alongside strikes on Aramco, and The Electronic Intifada reports Yemen landing blows on Saudi Arabia.",
    "The case against reading this as reflation rests on copper and the curve. Copper is down 2.4% over both the week and the month even as oil climbed 11%, and the 30-year minus 10-year spread is unchanged at 34 basis points over the month, so long yields are rising on inflation rather than on a stronger growth outlook. The dollar index sitting at the 93rd percentile of its 52-week range, up 1.7% on the month, completes the picture: capital is sheltering in dollars, not chasing cyclical exposure.",
    "Gold is the telling anomaly. It has fallen 6% over the month to the bottom sixth of its 52-week range while a shooting conflict escalates, which says real rates and the strong dollar are overwhelming safe-haven demand. The 7% monthly rise in gold ounces per bitcoin flatters bitcoin; it is really gold falling, since bitcoin itself is up under 1% on the month in a calm regime, with no sign of a liquidity-driven risk bid.",
    "The escalation channel is not fully priced. MintPress News reports Iran claiming it killed 200 US soldiers at bases in Jordan, a CENTCOM denial, and US threats to deploy its largest conventional bomb, yet Brent slipped 0.2% today and sits 8% below its 52-week high. Further attacks on Gulf infrastructure are bullish for Brent, supportive of the dollar, and put upward pressure on the 10-year and 30-year yields through the inflation channel; they are a headwind for copper as demand destruction bites, and gold is likely to stay capped until yields turn.",
    "Two tensions deserve weight. The 10-year yield fell 10 basis points this week while oil rose 4%, the first hint the market is pricing an oil-induced growth scare over inflation, and the Canadian dollar has failed to benefit from $109 oil, with USD/CAD up 1% on the month, consistent with dollar haven flows dominating commodity links. The rupee's 2% weekly gain against an oil-importer backdrop is an outlier that oil alone does not explain. The three-month trend, with yields up 54 basis points and Brent up 23%, still outweighs one week of respite."
  ],
